{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And you shall make two cherubim of gold.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the cherubim in the Temple and in the Tabernacle are a marvel and a testimony to the existence of angels. For just as we were commanded to believe in the existence of the blessed Name — and this is the first principle among the principles of the Torah, as it is written (Exodus 20:2): ״אנכי ה׳ אלהיך״ — so too were we commanded to believe in the existence of angels, and this is the second principle. For the angels are those who bestow the power of intellect and place speech in the mouths of the prophets by command of the blessed Name, for without this there is no prophecy, and if there is no prophecy there is no Torah. And because of this Scripture commanded the making of the cherubim to indicate the existence of angels. And the reason they were two and not one is so that you should not think it is an image of the deity that is worshipped. And if you would say: then you have reason to doubt two authorities — that is not so, for they were spreading their wings upward, receiving the influx of power that comes to them from above. This is the view of the Rambam, of blessed memory, concerning the matter of the cherubim, with some of his own language." "<b>And because</b> the cherubim, in my opinion, are an allusion to the existence of angels, and the angels are many, therefore we may say accordingly that the word כרובים comes to allude to the language of 'many' (רבים), and YHWH, the Elohim of Israel, is upon them, as He said (Psalms 80:2): ״יושב הכרובים״. If so, the word כרובים indicates two meanings: the language of 'many' and the language of 'youths' (נערים) from the language of רביא, and for both these senses the letter כ is added. And the reason for the addition is that the letter כ alludes to a throne (כסא), for indeed the name of the throne is established upon the letter כ for a hidden reason, and it also alludes to glory (כבוד). From this it will be understood that the angels are a throne of glory to receive the glory, for so it is written (Ezekiel 10:19): ״וכבוד אלהי ישראל עליהם מלמעלה״. And further we may say accordingly, that the reason the cherubim stood in a third of the Tabernacle — and likewise in the Temple, in a third of the House, as our Sages, of blessed memory, said at the beginning of the first chapter of Sukkah — and this is the lower third, was because the angel is a third of the world, that is, a third portion of the world. Therefore they were placed in the lower third, to clarify that even though the angels are a third portion and the upper one, they are nevertheless the lower one relative to the One who rides upon them, who is above them — unlike the mezuzah, which is placed in the upper third, for a reason I shall write when I arrive there, with the help of HaShem, blessed be He." "<b>And the reason</b> He mentioned in the command ״ועשית שנים כרובים זהב״ and did not say שני (two) is because שני is the language of equivalence, as in שני לוחות העדות (the two tablets of testimony), שני כבשים (two lambs), שני השעירים (the two goats). Therefore He needed to say שנים, for they are distinct in their nature, one male and one female. And He reverted and said ״מבין שני הכרובים״, and likewise ״ויעש שני כרובים זהב״, to allude to their equivalence in gold and in unity." "<b>And by way of the plain sense (peshat)</b> the two cherubim were male and female, to make known how beloved Israel is before the Holy One, blessed be He, like the love of male and female. And so our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded in tractate Yoma, chapter אמר להם הממונה: Rav Katina said, at the time when Israel ascended for the festival pilgrimage, they would draw back the curtain for them and show them the cherubim, which were intertwined one with the other, and say to them: 'See, your belovedness before HaShem is like the belovedness of male with female.' And so it is written (I Kings 7:36): ״כמער איש ולויות״ — Rabba bar Shila said: like a man intertwined with his wife (לויה)." "<b>And you must</b> reflect upon their statement: that the cherubim were in this form as a forceful parable to testify to the surpassing degree of attachment between the Holy One, blessed be He, and Israel, without any intermediary. For He, exalted be He, alone guides Israel, and no alien god is with Him — unlike the rest of the idolaters, to whom He appointed heavenly princes. And it is possible that one of the cherubim was in the form of a grown man and the second in the form of a small child, in the manner that Ezekiel saw: ״פני האחד פני הכרוב ופני השני פני אדם״, and our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: 'this one, a cherub; this one, a man' — rather, one had a large face (אפי רברבי) and one had a small face (אפי זוטרי). And this would testify to His love for Israel like the love of a father for his son, which is a strong love. But He wished to make a parable of physical attachment than which there is none greater, which is impossible through an intermediary, and it is stronger when it is from youth, as it is written (Proverbs 5:18): ״ושמח מאשת נעוריך״. And all this is so that we may know and understand that His attachment, exalted be He, with us is a great and strong attachment without any intermediary; and since His attachment with the rest of the idolaters is only by means of intermediaries, when He wished to inform us in the Torah that His attachment with us is without any intermediary, it was impossible to inform us except by means of this parable, of necessity." "<b>And further.</b> Another reason: so that we arouse ourselves in our prayers and in all the other forms of worship not to place any intermediary between the Holy One, blessed be He, and ourselves. For this reason it was placed in the innermost sanctuary, in the place of the Shekhinah — it is the pivot toward which all turn in prayer." "<b>And if</b> you examine the view of the Rambam, of blessed memory, concerning the matter of the cherubim, you will find that they correspond to the cherubim that Ezekiel the prophet, peace be upon him, saw, and they are the holy Ḥayyot. And even though he saw four Ḥayyot, they are all one Ḥayah. And from this Ezekiel mentioned (Ezekiel 10:20), ״היא החיה אשר ראיתי תחת אלהי ישראל בנהר כבר ואדע כי כרובים המה״ — after he had mentioned above four Ḥayyot, he afterward reduced them to one Ḥayah and said that they are the cherubim. And after he mentioned his first apprehension, which is the holy Ḥayyot, he returned and mentioned a second apprehension, which is the Ofannim, that are beside the Ḥayyot and below them, in the manner of what is written (ibid. 1): ״וארא החיות והנה אופן אחד בארץ אצל החיות״. And he had already mentioned in the first apprehension of the Ḥayyot: ״וארא והנה רוח סערה״ and so forth. And afterward he mentioned a third apprehension, the highest of all, which is the apprehension of the Ḥashmal, and said: ״וארא כעין חשמל״. And concerning these three apprehensions that he attained, he mentioned three times ״וארא״ — and this indicates that they are three distinct apprehensions, and they are, from below upward: Ofannim, Ḥayyot, and Ḥashmal. And these three apprehensions are what are called the Account of the Chariot (ma'aseh merkavah), for so they interpreted, the Rabbis, of blessed memory, in tractate Ḥagigah: Until where is the Account of the Chariot? Rabbi says: until the last ״וארא״. Rabbi Yitzḥak says: until ״חשמל״ — we teach the essence from here onward we transmit chapter-headings. Rabbi said: from ״וארא״ to ״חשמל״ we transmit chapter-headings; from here onward, if he was wise and understanding from his own knowledge — yes; if not — no. See that our holy Rabbi holds that the three apprehensions that Ezekiel attained are what are called the Account of the Chariot, and one may teach from them only chapter-headings. And there are those who say that the apprehension of Ḥashmal is not taught at all, not even by way of transmitting chapter-headings, unless he was wise and understanding from his own knowledge — yes; if not — no. And it was already explained above that the existence of the Ḥashmal is found from the flashes of the Throne of Glory. And you should know that it encompasses the Throne and generates the speech of the Glory to the holy Ḥayyot and to the angels and to all the hosts on high. And what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted — ״חשמל״ — חיות אש ממללות (Ḥayyot of fire speaking) — should not be understood from this that the Ḥashmal is the holy Ḥayyot; rather they explained to us the preeminence of the Ḥashmal, that the holy Ḥayyot are the influx of the Ḥashmal. And just as the holy Ḥayyot govern the Ofannim, for the Ofannim have no movement of themselves, so too the Ḥashmal governs the holy Ḥayyot and all their governance is through it, and through it the Ḥayyot are attentive to the sound of the speech. And on this account the Ḥashmal is so named to indicate its action, for it is the cause that the Ḥayyot are attentive (חשות) and hasten (ממהרות) — that is, it urges them, saying: ״Hasten (חשו) from the speaking (מן המלול) because of the voice of YHWH hewing flames of fire,״ and immediately the Ḥayyot leap like the appearance of a flash. And the sages who probe deeply into the secret of the Chariot said that the Ḥashmal contains three hundred and seventy-eight kinds of luminaries, equal to its numerical value, and the smallest of them all is like the brightness of the orb of the sun, and it transforms in a single moment into all the hues and into all those kinds of luminaries, and all of them are distinct forms, transforming in the likeness of male and female and joining with one another. And when they are not joined as one, they have no form. And it is investigated from the fourth Sefirah, which is Ḥesed, and its analogue among the lowly created beings, in the multiplicity of its hues, is the peacock, which has three hundred and sixty-five hues and kinds of colors, equal to the number of the days of the sun. And see, it lacks from the hues of the Ḥashmal thirteen hues; and were it not lacking these thirteen hues, whoever hears the voice of the peacock would be seized by a convulsion. And the cherubim were מקשה (beaten work from one piece) to indicate unity, for all four are one Ḥayah. And the reason they were of gold is that the holy Ḥayyot are of strong, mighty fire, and the appearance of gold is like the appearance of fire. And the reason they were two and not one — even though all is one Ḥayah — was a necessary matter, in order to indicate that they act in the world according to what they receive from above; and therefore it was necessary for them to be two, male and female, to indicate active and receptive, for they themselves are the active ones and the receptive ones." "<b>And this</b> is the order of the apprehension of the Chariot for Ezekiel: first he saw the holy Ḥayyot, corresponding to which are the cherubim in the Tabernacle and in the Temple; and afterward he saw the firmament stretched over the Ḥayyot; and afterward he saw the Throne upon the firmament. And the reason the cherubim were of the kapporet itself — מקשה — is that thus the fire of the Ḥayyot and the fire of the firmament are one, and all is one root; and therefore they were created on a single day, namely the second day of the creation of the world. And so the Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: the angels were created on the second day of the creation of the world, and the firmament was created on the second day, as it is said (Genesis 1:6): ״יהי רקיע״ — this is the firmament of the heads of the Ḥayyot." "<b>And you must</b> know that the holy Ḥayyot are in the seventh firmament, which is Aravot; and so the Rabbis, of blessed memory, mentioned in tractate Ḥagigah, that Aravot is the seventh." "<b>And some</b> of the masters of the Kabbalah said that above Aravot there is an eighth firmament whose name is Ma'on, and there are the rivers of joy, and therefore we say: ״the joy in His dwelling place (במעונו)״. And above it is a ninth firmament whose name is Makhon. And the three of them together — the seventh and the eighth and the ninth — are called Aravot. And so the holy Ḥayyot are in that seventh firmament whose name is Aravot; and the firmament whose name is Ma'on is stretched over their heads; and the Throne of Glory, upon which the Shekhinah rests, is the ninth, whose name is Makhon, also called Aravot. And this is what David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 68:5): ״סולו לרוכב בערבות ביה שמו״, and it is written (1 Kings 8:13): ״מכון לשבתך עולמים״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: the two cherubim in the Tabernacle and in the Temple correspond to the voice and the fire that were at Mount Sinai, mentioned in the verse (Deuteronomy 4) מן השמים וגו׳. And they were both of gold, for all receives from ḥesed and din. And they were beaten work (מקשה) to indicate unity. And they were male and female to indicate bestower and receiver. And they were — one cherub from one end and one cherub from the other end — for thus one is the head of the lower structure and the other its end, and this is the language of מן הכפורת תעשו את הכרובים על שני קצותיו. And the cherubim spread their wings upward in order to receive from above, and their faces were each toward his brother, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: when Israel do the will of the Omnipresent — אל הכפורת יהיו פני הכרובים, like a disciple who casts his eyes downward out of awe of his master. And understand this, for the cover (כפורת) with its cherubim alludes to five lower ones, which spread wings upward — five corresponding to five." "<b>And now</b> consider how pleasant and how good it is that the tablets of testimony should be beneath them in the Ark, for from them we heard the Torah, as it says (Deuteronomy 5:4) ביום דבר ה׳ עמכם בהר מתוך האש. And the fact that it repeated a second time, ואל הארון תתן את העדות אשר אתן אליך, is a warning that there should be no Ark without tablets, in the manner that they said concerning the Table, that the Table should not be left overnight without bread. For the tablets in the Ark allude to what is written (Isaiah 26) כי ביה ה׳, and likewise (Exodus 23:21) כי שמי בקרבו, and this is clear. And the cherubim that Ezekiel saw, which are the holy living creatures, correspond to them, and this is what it says (Ezekiel 10:20) ואדע כי כרובים המה — for since he saw the cherubim, which are the holy living creatures, he knew that above them were other cherubim that he did not see; the cherubim he saw were a likeness of them." "<b>And behold,</b> the cherubim that he saw and those in the Tabernacle and those in the Temple are all a likeness of those supernal cherubim that he knew, and they too are a throne of glory to receive the Supernal, and they are those of which the prophet said (Isaiah 64:10) בית קדשנו ותפארתנו אשר הללוך אבותינו. And you already know that the voice was heard by Moses from there, from between the two cherubim, within a span of a handbreadth (טפח), for there the Holy One, blessed be He, contracted His Shekhinah, even though it is written of Him (Jeremiah 23:24) הלא את השמים ואת הארץ אני מלא נאם ה׳." "<b>And likewise</b> you find in Midrash Tanchuma: Rabbi Yehoshua of Sikhnin said — a parable of a cave that is by the seashore: the sea surges, the cave is filled, and the sea lacks nothing. So too the Holy One, blessed be He, contracted His Shekhinah between the two cherubim, and despite this His glory is upon earth and heaven — end of quotation." "<b>And from the wisdom</b> of Formation (יצירה) in the pattern of man, you find that they traced in him three distinct organs, each one the measure of this contraction (צמצום), and in them are imprinted the י״ד and the ה״א and the וי״ו in the body of man from below upward, and the heart, which is also distinct, corresponds to the final ה״א. And understand this." "<b>And the Rabbis</b> in Bambar Rabba darshaned: the cover upon the Ark corresponds to the seraphim that are upon the Throne, as it says (Isaiah 6:2): ״שרפים עומדים ממעל לו״ — 'Seraphim stood above Him'; and they are the counselors of the Shekhinah. The two cherubim correspond to heaven and earth, in which is the seat of the Holy One, blessed be He. And understand this."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {3 "The phrase לויות is read by Rabba bar Shila as לויה, 'wife,' an interpretive play preserved in translation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:05:15.951544802Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}